The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
prev = req->timeout.head;
req->timeout.head = NULL;
if (prev && req_ref_inc_not_zero(prev))
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `prev` pointer is assigned the value of `req->timeout.head`, which can lead to a situation where a linked timeout is freed while still being referenced. If `req_ref_inc_not_zero(prev)` returns true, the code proceeds to remove the timeout from the list, but if the timeout has already been put in the fail path, it can lead to a use-after-free scenario when the memory is accessed again.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (prev) {
    io_remove_next_linked(prev);
    if (!req_ref_inc_not_zero(prev))
        prev = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that the linked timeout is only removed from the master request link list if it is still valid. By checking `if (prev)` and then calling `req_ref_inc_not_zero(prev)` within the same conditional block, it prevents the race condition that could lead to a use-after-free. If the reference count is zero, `prev` is set to `NULL`, ensuring that no further operations are attempted on a potentially freed timeout.

